

# 7. The dev loop

There is no `crab dev`. There will not be one.

```sh
turborust up
```

## Why not

Writing a dev server is easy. Writing a *good* one — file watching that knows
about a Cargo path-dependency closure, caching that survives `git checkout`,
live reload, and build errors shown where you are looking rather than in a
terminal behind the browser — is a project, and it is not this project.

[turborust](https://github.com/oddurs/turborust) is that project. It watches
by asking `cargo metadata` for a crate's dependency closure, so editing your
design system rebuilds the site without anyone having to remember to list the
path. It caches on content hashes rather than modification times. It serves
`dist/` with live reload. And it parses rustc diagnostics out of cargo's
output and puts them in a browser overlay, with a code frame and an editor
link, over a blur of the page you were reading.

A second, worse version of that inside crabbucket would be the least
defensible thing in the tree.

## The config

`crab new` writes this for you. It lives at the repository root:

```toml
[overlay]
position = "bottom-right"
emoji = "🦀"
theme = "auto"
errors = "overlay"

[tasks.site]
cargo = "crabbucket-cli"
cmd = "crab build ."

[services.web]
depends_on = ["site"]
serve = { dir = "dist", port = 8790 }
```

Three blocks. What to run, what to serve, and where to put the errors.

For a site that is a crate — everything from [chapter
5](../your-own-design-system/) onwards — the command is `cargo run` instead,
and turborust picks up the design system as a watched path automatically
because it is a dependency.

## If you do not want turborust

Nothing requires it. `crab build` produces a directory; serve it with
whatever you like:

```sh
crab build . && python3 -m http.server -d dist 8000
```

You lose the watching and the error overlay. You keep everything else,
because the output is a directory of static files with no server
requirements — which is also why the [deploy](../deploying/) is boring.

## The thing that will surprise you

The build is fast, but it is not instantaneous, and the reason is
[measured](~/docs/speed/): syntax highlighting is about sixty percent of it.
A page with no code blocks rebuilds in a few milliseconds; a docs site full
of Rust takes a few hundred.

Social cards are the other one. Drawing them is the most expensive thing a
crabbucket build does — about 1.4ms per page — so they are cached in
`.crabbucket/`. Keep that directory between builds and you will not notice
them. Delete it and you will.

